DECIDE `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted of taking a conveyance without authority` IS
    `s 12(2) liable on summary conviction for taking a conveyance without authority` `my conveyance facts`
    WHERE
        `my conveyance facts` MEANS `Conveyance facts` WITH
            `constructed or adapted for the carriage of a person or persons by land, water or air` IS `it is built or adapted to carry a person or people by land, water or air`
            `constructed or adapted for use only under the control of a person not carried in or on it` IS `it is built or adapted for use only under the control of someone who is not carried in or on it`
            `it is a pedal cycle` IS `it is a pedal cycle`
            `it is the subject of a hiring agreement or hire-purchase agreement` IS `it is the subject of a hiring or hire-purchase agreement`
            `the owner consented` IS `the owner consented`
            `the person in possession under the hiring or hire-purchase agreement consented` IS `the person in possession under the hiring or hire-purchase agreement consented`
            `he had other lawful authority` IS `I had other lawful authority`
            `took it for his own or another's use` IS `I took it for my own or another's use`
            `drove it` IS `I drove it`
            `allowed himself to be carried in or on it` IS `I allowed myself to be carried in or on it`
            -- Placeholder: the rules called never read this field.
            `rode it` IS FALSE
            `knew it had been taken without such consent or authority` IS `I knew it had been taken without such consent or authority`
            `believed he had lawful authority to do what he did` IS `I believed I had lawful authority to do what I did`
            `believed he would have the owner's consent if the owner knew of his doing it and the circumstances` IS `I believed the owner would have consented if he knew`

-- s 12(4). On a trial on indictment for theft, if the jury are not satisfied that the accused is guilty of
-- theft but it is proved that he committed an offence under s 12(1), the jury may find him guilty of the
-- s 12(1) offence.
@export May the jury find me guilty of taking a conveyance without authority on a charge of theft (s 12(4))?
@desc Whether, on the facts you state, the jury may return a verdict of guilty of the section 12(1) offence on a trial for theft.
GIVEN `I am being tried on indictment for theft` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you being tried on indictment for theft?
      `the jury are not satisfied that I committed theft` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the jury not satisfied that you committed theft?
      `it is proved that I committed an offence under section 12(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it proved that you committed an offence under section 12(1) (taking a conveyance without authority)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the jury find me guilty of taking a conveyance without authority on a charge of theft` IS
    `s 12(4) the jury may find him guilty of taking a conveyance without authority` `the verdict facts`
    WHERE
        `the verdict facts` MEANS `Alternative verdict facts` WITH
            `tried on indictment for theft` IS `I am being tried on indictment for theft`
            `the jury are not satisfied the accused committed theft` IS `the jury are not satisfied that I committed theft`
            `it is proved the accused committed an offence under section 12(1)` IS `it is proved that I committed an offence under section 12(1)`

@export What follows if the jury find me guilty of taking a conveyance without authority on a charge of theft (s 12(4))?
@desc The most that can follow on that verdict, which is the same as for the offence tried on its own.
GIVEN `I am being tried on indictment for theft` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you being tried on indictment for theft?
      `the jury are not satisfied that I committed theft` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the jury not satisfied that you committed theft?
      `it is proved that I committed an offence under section 12(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it proved that you committed an offence under section 12(1) (taking a conveyance without authority)?
GIVETH AN `Outcome of a charge`
DECIDE `What follows if the jury find me guilty of taking a conveyance without authority on a charge of theft` IS
    `s 12(4) liable as under subsection (2) on a verdict returned on a charge of theft` `the verdict facts`
    WHERE
        `the verdict facts` MEANS `Alternative verdict facts` WITH
            `tried on indictment for theft` IS `I am being tried on indictment for theft`
            `the jury are not satisfied the accused committed theft` IS `the jury are not satisfied that I committed theft`
            `it is proved the accused committed an offence under section 12(1)` IS `it is proved that I committed an offence under section 12(1)`

-- s 12(4A) to (4C). Proceedings for the s 12(1) offence against a mechanically propelled vehicle (other
-- than on the trial of an indictment for theft) must be commenced within three years of the offence and within
-- six months of the day the prosecutor first had enough evidence. Where another person prosecutes after a
-- public prosecution was discontinued, the later of two dates starts the six months.
@export Is it too late to bring proceedings against me for taking a vehicle without authority (s 12(4A))?
@desc Whether section 12(4A) allows proceedings to be commenced on the dates you give, bars them, or sets no limit on proceedings of this kind.
GIVEN `the vehicle is mechanically propelled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the vehicle mechanically propelled?
      `the proceedings are the kind in section 12(4), on a trial for theft` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the proceedings the kind described in section 12(4), that is on the trial of an indictment for theft?
      `the day the offence was committed` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the offence committed?
      `who is bringing the prosecution` IS A `Who prosecutes` @desc Who is bringing the prosecution?
      `the day sufficient evidence came to the knowledge of the person who decides whether to prosecute` IS A DATE @desc On what day did sufficient evidence to justify the proceedings come to the knowledge of the person who decides whether to prosecute? (If the prosecutor has a certificate of that date, give the date it certifies: it is conclusive under section 12(4C).)
      `the day the earlier public prosecution was discontinued, if there was one` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc If a public prosecution on the same facts was discontinued, on what day was it? (Leave this out if there was none.)
      `the day the proceedings were commenced` IS A DATE @desc On what day were the proceedings commenced?
GIVETH A `Time limit position`
DECIDE `Is it too late to bring proceedings against me for taking a vehicle without authority` IS
    `s 12(4A) the time limit for proceedings` `the time facts`
    WHERE
        `the time facts` MEANS `Time limit facts` WITH
            `the vehicle is mechanically propelled` IS `the vehicle is mechanically propelled`
            `the proceedings are of the kind described in section 12(4)` IS `the proceedings are the kind in section 12(4), on a trial for theft`
            `the date the offence was committed` IS `the day the offence was committed`
            `who is prosecuting` IS `who is bringing the prosecution`
            `the date sufficient evidence to justify the proceedings came to the knowledge of the person section 12(4B) names` IS `the day sufficient evidence came to the knowledge of the person who decides whether to prosecute`
            `the date the earlier public prosecution was discontinued` IS `the day the earlier public prosecution was discontinued, if there was one`
            `the date the proceedings were commenced` IS `the day the proceedings were commenced`

§§ `Section 12(5) -- pedal cycles`

