§§ `Section 32 -- effect on existing law and powers`

-- s 32(1). The offences of larceny, robbery, burglary, receiving stolen property, obtaining property by threats, extortion by colour of
-- office or franchise, false accounting by public officers, concealment of treasure trove, and (except where it relates to the public
-- revenue) cheating, and the Schedule 3 Part I offences to the extent stated there, are abolished for all purposes not relating to
-- offences committed before the commencement of this Act.
@export Is the old offence abolished as regards what I did (s 32(1))?
@desc Whether, on the facts you state, the old common-law or statutory offence is abolished as regards what you did.
GIVEN `the old offence` IS AN `Abolished offence` @desc Which of the old offences is it? Choose the one that fits.
      `the last day on which any part of the offence was committed` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the last part of the offence committed?
      `the cheating relates to the public revenue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it is cheating, does it relate to the public revenue?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the old offence abolished as regards what I did` IS
    `s 32(1) the old offence is abolished as regards what he did` `the abolition facts`
    WHERE
        `the abolition facts` MEANS `Abolition facts` WITH
            `the old offence` IS `the old offence`
            `the last day on which any part of the offence was committed` IS `the last day on which any part of the offence was committed`
            `the cheating relates to the public revenue` IS `the cheating relates to the public revenue`

§§ `Section 35 -- commencement`

-- s 35(1). This Act came into force on 1 January 1969.
@export When did the Theft Act 1968 come into force (s 35(1))?
@desc The date the Act came into force.
-- (no inputs)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `When did the Theft Act 1968 come into force` IS
    `s 35(1) the date the Act came into force`

-- The Act was passed on 26 July 1968 (the date under the long title).
@export When was the Theft Act 1968 passed?
@desc The date the Act was passed, shown under its long title.
-- (no inputs)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `When was the Theft Act 1968 passed` IS
    `the date the Theft Act 1968 was enacted`

-- s 35(1). The Act has effect in relation to offences wholly or partly committed on or after the day it came into force.
@export Does the Theft Act 1968 apply to what I did (s 35(1))?
@desc Whether the Act has effect in relation to an offence whose last part was committed on the day you give.
GIVEN `the last day any part of what I did happened` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the last part of what you are accused of happen?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Theft Act 1968 apply to what I did` IS
    `s 35(1) the Act has effect in relation to an offence` `the last day any part of what I did happened`

-- s 35(2). Section 27 applies to proceedings for an offence committed before the commencement as it would to proceedings for a
-- corresponding offence under this Act.
@export Does section 27 apply to the offence I am accused of committing before the Act came into force (s 35(2))?
@desc Whether section 27 applies to an offence committed on the day you give, because that day is before the Act came into force.
GIVEN `the day I am accused of committing the offence` IS A DATE @desc On what day are you accused of committing the offence?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does section 27 apply to the offence I am accused of committing before the Act came into force` IS
    `s 35(2) section 27 applies to the offence as it would to a corresponding offence under this Act` `the day I am accused of committing the offence`

§§ `Section 36 -- short title, extent`

-- s 36(3). This Act does not extend to Scotland or Northern Ireland. England and Wales is within it by the long title. For the Isle of Man,
-- the Channel Islands and elsewhere the text says nothing.
@export Does the Theft Act 1968 extend to where I am (s 36(3))?
@desc Whether the Act extends to the place you choose, or does not, or the text does not say.
GIVEN `where I am` IS A `Jurisdiction` @desc Which of these places are you in?
GIVETH AN `Extent of the Act`
DECIDE `Does the Theft Act 1968 extend to where I am` IS
    `s 36(3) where the Act extends` `where I am`

§§ `Schedule 1 -- taking or destroying fish`

-- Sch 1 para 2(1). A person who unlawfully takes or destroys, or attempts to take or destroy, any fish in water which is private property or
-- in which there is a private right of fishery is guilty of an offence.
@export Am I guilty of unlawfully taking or destroying fish (Sch 1 para 2(1))?
@desc Whether, on the facts you state, you are guilty of unlawfully taking or destroying fish in private water.
GIVEN `I unlawfully took or destroyed fish, or tried to` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you unlawfully take or destroy fish, or attempt to?
      `the fish were in water which is private property or in which there is a private right of fishery` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the fish in water which is private property or in which there is a private right of fishery?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I guilty of unlawfully taking or destroying fish` IS
    `Sch 1 para 2(1) guilty of unlawfully taking or destroying fish` `my fish facts`
    WHERE
        `my fish facts` MEANS `Fish facts` WITH
            `he unlawfully took or destroyed fish, or attempted to` IS `I unlawfully took or destroyed fish, or tried to`
            `the fish were in water which is private property or in which there is a private right of fishery` IS `the fish were in water which is private property or in which there is a private right of fishery`
            -- Placeholder: the rules called never read this field.
            `he has been convicted of that offence` IS FALSE
